TCP command handlers + tcp_get_info introspection
=================================================

The four TCP IPC commands (`TCP_CONNECT`, `TCP_SEND`, `TCP_RECV`,
`TCP_CLOSE`) plus their sibling handlers (`TCP_DEBUG`,
`TCP_RECV_TIMEOUT`, `TCP_GET_INFO`, listen / accept / shutdown,
NB-config injection, blackhole arm, keepalive controls). All are
blocking on the wire — handlers park the caller in a per-socket
waiter slot and the TCP state-machine / timer scan delivers the
reply later. SEND is the exception (POSIX `write()` semantics →
immediate ack once bytes land in the send buffer).

`inet_handle_tcp_get_info` populates a Linux-compatible 104-byte
`struct tcp_info` (RTT in microseconds, etc.) so userspace can use
the introspection ABI without translation.

Same `oskit.servers` module as `inet.lsysl`, so sibling state
(`inet_sockets`, `tcp_sockets`, the TCP state machine in tcp.lsysl,
TCP option parsers in tcp.lsysl, all socket-table helpers in
udp_hash.lsysl) resolves without imports.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{ipc_reply}
    import oskit.services.{monotonic_ms}
    import std.alloc.{free}


### TCP command handlers

All four commands are blocking: they do NOT reply before the
wakeup condition is met. Handlers stash the caller tid in the
per-socket waiter slot (connect_waiter / recv_waiter / close_waiter)
and the state machine / timer scan delivers the reply later.
SEND is the one exception — POSIX `write()` semantics mean it
replies as soon as bytes land in the send buffer.

Wire layout (all big-endian u16):
- TCP_CONNECT: cmd(1), ip(4), port(2) -> status(1)
- TCP_SEND: cmd(1), fd(2), len(2), data(len) -> status(1), written(2)
- TCP_RECV: cmd(1), fd(2), maxlen(2) -> status(1), len(2), data(len); len=0 means EOF
- TCP_CLOSE: cmd(1), fd(2) -> status(1)

`fd` is a 16-bit raw socket index. First byte at offset 1 is the
high byte (0), second (offset 2) is the low byte / index.

    inet_read_u16_at(buf: *byte, off: int) -> int
        inet_read_u16(buf, off)

    inet_handle_tcp_connect(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 1)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 9)
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_connect(sender, sender, ip, port, sndbuf, rcvbuf)
        if s < 0
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        // Deferred reply — inet_tcp_state_step replies when ESTABLISHED.

Non-blocking connect. Allocate slot + emit SYN like the
blocking variant, but pass `-1` for `caller_tid` so the slot's
connect_waiter is the unset sentinel and the SYN-ACK path
won't try to deliver an IPC reply. Reply immediately with the
slot fd; the shim translates that to `-EINPROGRESS` for the
user. EPOLLOUT delivery once ESTABLISHED comes through the
existing `inet_epoll_fire(2, s)` at SYN_SENT → ESTABLISHED.

    inet_handle_tcp_connect_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 1)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 9)
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_connect(sender, -1, ip, port, sndbuf, rcvbuf)
        var reply: [2]byte
        if s < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        reply[1] = byte(s & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)

    inet_handle_tcp_send(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val dlen = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        // SO_SNDTIMEO arms the parked-waiter deadline; 0 means
        // "no timeout" (Linux default — park indefinitely).
        val timeout_ms = inet_read_u16(buf, 5) & 0xffff
        val data: *byte = buf + 7
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val n = inet_tcp_do_send(fd, data, dlen)
        if n < 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        // Park caller when send_buf is full and they wanted bytes.
        // Recv loop stays unblocked; ACK drain wakes via
        // inet_tcp_drain_send_waiter with reply (status=0,
        // written=0).  Shim retries the same chunk against the
        // freshly opened window.  Single-waiter for now —
        // concurrent senders on the same fd reply 0 immediately
        // and the shim retries (busy-loop, but only when two
        // threads share an fd, which POSIX rarely promises).
        if n == 0 && dlen > 0 && tcp_sockets[fd].send_waiter < 0
            tcp_sockets[fd].send_waiter = sender
            tcp_sockets[fd].send_waiter_len = dlen
            // SO_SNDTIMEO deadline lives in `monotonic_ms` units —
            // same convention as INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT and the
            // scan_timers comparison.  0 disables the deadline.
            if timeout_ms > 0
                tcp_sockets[fd].send_deadline = int(monotonic_ms()) + timeout_ms
            else
                tcp_sockets[fd].send_deadline = 0
            return
        var reply: [3]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, n)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)

    inet_handle_tcp_recv(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > 500
            maxlen = 500
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
        tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
        if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
            var give = tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len
            if give > maxlen
                give = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, give)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, 0)
            return
        // Deferred reply — state step delivers when data arrives.

Non-blocking TCP recv. Same wire format as TCP_RECV (cmd + fd +
maxlen). Three reply shapes:
  - data ready → status=0 + len + data (same as TCP_RECV).
  - peer FIN  → status=0 + len=0 (EOF, same as TCP_RECV).
  - no data   → status=-11 (-EAGAIN, 3 bytes).
Invalid fd / closed socket still produces the -1 error reply.
Unlike TCP_RECV this never parks the caller; the shim picks
the NB variant when `O_NONBLOCK` is set on the fd.

    inet_handle_tcp_recv_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > 500
            maxlen = 500
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
            var give = tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len
            if give > maxlen
                give = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, give)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, 0)
            return
        var eagain: [3]byte
        eagain[0] = byte(-11)
        inet_write_u16(eagain, 1, 0)
        ipc_reply(sender, eagain, 3)

Non-consuming readiness query for the shim's epoll
implementation. Caller passes (kind, fd) and gets back a
`ready_mask` packed into the reply: bit 0 = readable, bit 2
= writable, bit 3 = error, bit 4 = hangup. Same bit positions
as Linux's `EPOLLIN` / `EPOLLOUT` / `EPOLLERR` / `EPOLLHUP`.

Wire: [cmd(1), kind(1), fd(u16)] = 4 bytes.
Reply: [status(1), mask(u16), fire_seq(u32 le)] = 7 bytes.

`fire_seq` is the per-fd monotonic counter that
`inet_epoll_fire` bumps on every readiness edge — the shim
caches it per `epoll_table` entry and clears that entry's
`last_reported` only when the seq advances, so EPOLLET
subscribers don't over-fire on unrelated fds in the same
instance.

kind: 1 = UDP, 2 = TCP (listen sockets are detected by state).

For UDP, readable iff there's a queued datagram; writable
always. For TCP regular sockets, readable iff recv_len > 0 or
peer_fin (so an EOF read won't block); writable iff there is
send-buffer space and we're connected; hangup iff peer_fin.
For TCP listen sockets, readable iff the accept queue has a
ready child.

    inet_handle_poll(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val kind = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 2) & 0xffff
        var mask = 0
        var seq = 0
        if kind == 1
            // UDP — owner check via inet_fd_valid which scopes
            // by sender PTBR/tid.
            if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) != 0
                if inet_sockets[fd].q_count > 0
                    mask = mask | 0x1
                mask = mask | 0x4
                seq = inet_udp_fire_seq[fd]
        else if kind == 2
            if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) != 0
                val st = tcp_sockets[fd].state
                if st == TCP_STATE_LISTEN
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].accept_head != tcp_sockets[fd].accept_tail
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                else
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                        mask = mask | 0x10
                    if st == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED || st == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
                        if tcp_sockets[fd].send_len < tcp_sockets[fd].send_buf_size
                            mask = mask | 0x4
                    if st == TCP_STATE_CLOSED
                        mask = mask | 0x8
                        mask = mask | 0x10
                seq = tcp_sockets[fd].fire_seq
        var reply: [7]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, mask)
        // fire_seq little-endian (matches the shim's epoll-event
        // serialization throughout `oskit.posix.shim`).
        reply[3] = byte(seq & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte((seq >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[5] = byte((seq >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[6] = byte((seq >> 24) & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)

shutdown(fd, how). `how` values: 0 = SHUT_RD, 1 = SHUT_WR,
2 = SHUT_RDWR. SHUT_RD is a no-op today — we don't track a
separate "read shut down" state, so the peer sees no effect and
subsequent recvs still deliver data. SHUT_WR emits our FIN via
`inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr` without parking the caller. SHUT_RDWR
is treated as SHUT_WR (a proper SHUT_RDWR would also drop
buffered recv; deferred). Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16) + how(u8) = 4.
Reply: status(1).

    inet_handle_tcp_shutdown(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val how = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if how != 0
            val _ = inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr(fd)
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_tcp_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        // Phase 2A: drop sender from the owners ring. If another tid
        // still references this slot (transplanted or SCM-passed),
        // keep it alive — peer connection survives the partial close.
        val left = inet_tcp_owners_remove(fd, sender)
        if left > 0
            var ack: [1]byte
            ack[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, ack, 1)
            return
        // Phase 2B chunk 3: defer the FIN handshake while in-flight
        // SCM_RIGHTS still holds a queue ref. The receiver-side
        // QUEUE_REF(-1) drops the slot through `inet_tcp_close_slot`,
        // which is ungraceful for a connection that the sender has
        // already orphaned — acceptable, since the sender called
        // close() and the peer learns of the tear-down via RST.
        if tcp_sockets[fd].queued_refs > 0
            var ack: [1]byte
            ack[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, ack, 1)
            return
        val r = inet_tcp_do_close(fd, sender)
        if r == 1
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if r < 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        // Deferred reply — state step delivers when FIN is acked.

SO_KEEPALIVE on/off.  Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + on(1) = 4 bytes
in; reply: 1-byte status.  Inet enforces the probe/giveup cycle in
`inet_tcp_scan_timers` for sockets that are in ESTABLISHED or
CLOSE_WAIT with `keepalive_on != 0`.

    inet_handle_tcp_set_keepalive(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val on = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_set_keepalive(fd, on)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


setsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_KEEPIDLE/INTVL/CNT) — set a per-socket
override.  Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + kind(1) + value(u32 BE)
= 8 bytes in; reply: 1-byte status.  `kind` = 0 (idle), 1 (intvl),
2 (cnt).  Idle/intvl arrive as ticks (shim has converted from
seconds at the boundary); cnt is a raw count.

    inet_handle_tcp_set_keepopt(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val kind = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        val v_hi = inet_read_u16(buf, 4)
        val v_lo = inet_read_u16(buf, 6)
        val value = (v_hi << 16) | v_lo
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_set_keepopt(fd, kind, value)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


getsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_KEEPIDLE/INTVL/CNT) — read the per-socket
override.  Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + kind(1) = 4 bytes in;
reply: status(1) + value(u32 BE) = 5 bytes.

    inet_handle_tcp_get_keepopt(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val kind = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [5]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
            return
        val v = inet_tcp_get_keepopt(fd, kind)
        if v < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[3] = byte((v >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte( v        & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)


Read-and-clear the asynchronous-error channel for SO_ERROR.  Wire:
`cmd(1) + fd(u16) = 3` bytes.  Reply: `status(1) + errno(u8) = 2`
bytes — 0 errno means "no pending error".

This intentionally bypasses `inet_tcp_slot_owned`'s `in_use=0`
check: by the time the shim queries us, `close_slot` has almost
always already torn the slot down (that's the whole reason the
field exists). We still gate on `inet_tcp_owners_was_last(fd,
sender)` — i.e. `sender` is the slot's primary owner per
`owners[0]`, which Phase 2A's `owners_remove` deliberately leaves
intact when the ring drops to count=0. That keeps the original
"only the original owner can read another's pending error"
guarantee, plus admits any tid still in the ring while the slot
is in use (multi-owner via SCM_RIGHTS).

    inet_handle_tcp_get_err(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [2]byte
        if fd < 0 || fd >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = byte(0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        // Either still-live multi-owner or the original primary
        // surviving close — both legitimate for SO_ERROR access.
        var ok = inet_tcp_owners_has(fd, sender)
        if ok == 0
            ok = inet_tcp_owners_was_last(fd, sender)
        if ok == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = byte(0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        val e = tcp_sockets[fd].pending_error
        tcp_sockets[fd].pending_error = 0
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        reply[1] = byte(e & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)


Test-only: synthesize a SYN_SENT failure on whichever in_use slot
is currently waiting on `remote_port`.  Sets `pending_error = 111`
and runs `close_slot`, mirroring what an inbound RST in SYN_SENT
would do — without depending on slirp's RST timing.  Wire:
`cmd(1) + remote_port_be(u16) = 3` bytes.  Reply: 1-byte status
(0 = found-and-injected, -1 = no slot matched).

### inet_handle_tcp_get_info

Populates a Linux-compatible `struct tcp_info` (104 bytes) for
the slot. Field offsets and units (microseconds for RTT) match
what `getsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_INFO)` returns on Linux so
libuv / Go-net-stack diagnostics that probe specific offsets
keep working. Fields we don't track (delivery-rate, fackets,
last_data_*, pmtu, rcv_rtt, etc.) stay zero.

State enum is mapped from our local `TCP_STATE_*` values
(0 = CLOSED, 1 = SYN_SENT, ...) to Linux's
(1 = ESTABLISHED, 2 = SYN_SENT, ...). The shim doesn't try to
interpret it — it's a number the user reads.

RTT/RTTvar conversion: our `srtt` / `rttvar` live in scheduler
ticks (10 ms). Linux reports tcpi_rtt in microseconds, so we
multiply by 10000.

Wire: cmd(1) + fd_be(2) = 3 bytes. Reply: status(1) + 104-byte
struct = 105 bytes. Status -1 if fd out of range or not owned.

    inet_handle_tcp_get_info(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [105]byte
        var z = 0
        while z < 105
            reply[z] = byte(0)
            z += 1
        if fd < 0 || fd >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 105)
            return
        if inet_tcp_owners_has(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 105)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].in_use == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 105)
            return
        // Map slix TCP_STATE_* → Linux's struct tcp_info state.
        var lstate = 0
        val s = tcp_sockets[fd].state
        if s == TCP_STATE_CLOSED
            lstate = 7
        else if s == TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
            lstate = 2
        else if s == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
            lstate = 1
        else if s == TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_1
            lstate = 4
        else if s == TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_2
            lstate = 5
        else if s == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
            lstate = 8
        else if s == TCP_STATE_LAST_ACK
            lstate = 9
        else if s == TCP_STATE_TIME_WAIT
            lstate = 6
        else if s == TCP_STATE_LISTEN
            lstate = 10
        else if s == TCP_STATE_SYN_RCVD
            lstate = 3
        reply[0] = byte(0)                          // status
        // struct tcp_info begins at reply[1].  Offsets below are
        // relative to the struct base.
        reply[1 + 0] = byte(lstate & 0xff)          // tcpi_state @ 0
        reply[1 + 1] = byte(0)                      // tcpi_ca_state @ 1 (open)
        var rxmit = tcp_sockets[fd].retries
        if rxmit > 255
            rxmit = 255
        reply[1 + 2] = byte(rxmit & 0xff)           // tcpi_retransmits @ 2
        var probes = tcp_sockets[fd].keepalive_probes
        if probes > 255
            probes = 255
        reply[1 + 3] = byte(probes & 0xff)          // tcpi_probes @ 3
        reply[1 + 4] = byte(0)                      // tcpi_backoff @ 4
        // tcpi_options bitmap: bit 0 = TCPI_OPT_TIMESTAMPS,
        // bit 2 = TCPI_OPT_SACK, bit 3 = TCPI_OPT_WSCALE.
        var opts = 0
        if tcp_sockets[fd].ts_ok != 0
            opts = opts | 1
        if tcp_sockets[fd].sack_ok != 0
            opts = opts | 2
        if tcp_sockets[fd].snd_wscale != 0 || tcp_sockets[fd].rcv_wscale != 0
            opts = opts | 4
        reply[1 + 5] = byte(opts & 0xff)            // tcpi_options @ 5
        // Wscales packed: low 4 bits = snd, high 4 bits = rcv.
        val wscales = ((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_wscale & 0xf) | ((tcp_sockets[fd].rcv_wscale & 0xf) << 4))
        reply[1 + 6] = byte(wscales & 0xff)         // tcpi_*_wscale @ 6
        reply[1 + 7] = byte(0)                      // delivery_rate_app_limited @ 7
        // u32 fields. Inline little-endian writes — both arches
        // we target are LE and Linux's struct tcp_info is host-
        // endian, so this matches what real Linux apps expect.
        // tcpi_rto @ 8: our rto is in milliseconds; Linux uses
        // microseconds.  1ms = 1000us.
        val rto_us = tcp_sockets[fd].rto * 1000
        reply[1 +  8] = byte(rto_us & 0xff)
        reply[1 +  9] = byte((rto_us >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 10] = byte((rto_us >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 11] = byte((rto_us >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_ato @ 12 — delayed-ack timeout. We don't do
        // delayed ACKs, leave 0.
        // tcpi_snd_mss @ 16
        val smss = tcp_sockets[fd].mss
        reply[1 + 16] = byte(smss & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 17] = byte((smss >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 18] = byte((smss >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 19] = byte((smss >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_rcv_mss @ 20 — same.
        reply[1 + 20] = byte(smss & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 21] = byte((smss >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 22] = byte((smss >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 23] = byte((smss >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_unacked @ 24 — bytes in flight.
        val unacked = int(i32(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_nxt - tcp_sockets[fd].snd_una))
        reply[1 + 24] = byte(unacked & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 25] = byte((unacked >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 26] = byte((unacked >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 27] = byte((unacked >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_retrans @ 36 — retransmits in current burst.
        reply[1 + 36] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 37] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 38] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 39] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_rtt @ 68 in microseconds, tcpi_rttvar @ 72.
        val rtt_us = tcp_sockets[fd].srtt * 10000
        reply[1 + 68] = byte(rtt_us & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 69] = byte((rtt_us >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 70] = byte((rtt_us >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 71] = byte((rtt_us >> 24) & 0xff)
        val rttvar_us = tcp_sockets[fd].rttvar * 10000
        reply[1 + 72] = byte(rttvar_us & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 73] = byte((rttvar_us >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 74] = byte((rttvar_us >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 75] = byte((rttvar_us >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_snd_ssthresh @ 76, tcpi_snd_cwnd @ 80.
        reply[1 + 76] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 77] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 78] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 79] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 80] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 81] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 82] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 83] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_advmss @ 84 — same as snd_mss for us.
        reply[1 + 84] = byte(smss & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 85] = byte((smss >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 86] = byte((smss >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 87] = byte((smss >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_total_retrans @ 100
        reply[1 + 100] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 101] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 102] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 103] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 24) & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 105)


    inet_handle_tcp_inject_fail(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rport = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [1]byte
        var i = 0
        var hit = -1
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if tcp_sockets[i].state == TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
                    if tcp_sockets[i].remote_port == rport
                        hit = i
                        i = TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            i += 1
        if hit < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        tcp_sockets[hit].pending_error = 111  // ECONNREFUSED
        inet_tcp_close_slot(hit, -1)
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


setsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_USER_TIMEOUT) — write the per-socket
cap.  Wire: `cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + timeout_ms(u32 BE) = 7` bytes
in; reply: 1-byte status.  Inet stores milliseconds directly in
the `monotonic_ms` clock domain — a 0 timeout means "disable"
and is preserved as 0.

    inet_handle_tcp_set_userto(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val ms_hi = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        val ms_lo = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val timeout_ms = (ms_hi << 16) | ms_lo
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_set_userto(fd, timeout_ms)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


Test-only: arm/disarm the per-slot ACK blackhole used by the
TCP_USER_TIMEOUT smoke test.  Wire: `cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + on(1)
= 4` bytes in; reply: 1-byte status.  Bypasses the usual owner
check so a single-process test can flip the flag on a child slot
created by `accept()` (children inherit the parent's owner_tid,
so the check would pass anyway, but skipping it keeps the
injector independent of slot-ownership invariants).

    inet_handle_tcp_blackhole_acks_test(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val on = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if fd < 0 || fd >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].in_use == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_set_ack_blackhole(fd, on)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


SO_LINGER `l_linger = 0` close path.  Shim sends this when the
caller has set linger-on with a zero timeout: emit RST, free
slot, no FIN, no TIME_WAIT.  Reply is immediate (1-byte status)
since no FIN handshake happens.

    inet_handle_tcp_abort_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        // Phase 2A: linger=0 abort still has to wait for the last
        // owner before sending RST. Earlier closes just drop the
        // closer from the owners ring — they don't get to RST a
        // socket another tid still references.
        val left = inet_tcp_owners_remove(fd, sender)
        if left > 0
            reply[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        // Phase 2B chunk 3: defer the RST while in-flight SCM_RIGHTS
        // still holds a queue ref. The QUEUE_REF(-1) path will free
        // the slot ungracefully then — the RST opportunity is lost,
        // but linger=0 already chose the brutal path and the peer
        // sees the connection drop one way or another.
        if tcp_sockets[fd].queued_refs > 0
            reply[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_abort(fd)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_tcp_listen(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 1)
        val backlog = inet_read_u16(buf, 3) & 0xffff
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        // SO_REUSEADDR pre-armed on the shim fd; passed through so
        // `inet_tcp_begin_listen` can override the TIME_WAIT
        // bind-block check. Older shims sent a 9-byte LISTEN
        // message; sender always pads to MAX_IPC_PAYLOAD so the
        // byte at offset 9 is well-defined zero (= reuseaddr=0,
        // strict-bind), preserving the prior permissive behavior
        // for any pre-bump caller.
        val reuseaddr = int(buf[9]) & 0xff
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_listen(sender, port, backlog, sndbuf, rcvbuf, reuseaddr)
        var reply: [2]byte
        if s < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(s & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)

    inet_handle_tcp_accept(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        val child = inet_tcp_do_accept(sender, fd)
        if child == -2
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        if child >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_accept(sender, 0, child)
            return
        // child == -1 → caller parked; state_step will reply once a
        // SYN_RCVD child reaches ESTABLISHED.

Non-blocking accept. Identical wire to `INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT` but
never parks the caller — replies -EAGAIN if the queue is empty
so an event-loop client can drive accept() entirely from epoll
notifications. The shim picks this variant when the listen fd
has `O_NONBLOCK` set.

    inet_handle_tcp_accept_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        val child = inet_tcp_try_accept(fd)
        if child == -2
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        if child >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_accept(sender, 0, child)
            return
        // Queue empty — return -EAGAIN without parking.
        var eagain: [8]byte
        eagain[0] = byte(-11)
        ipc_reply(sender, eagain, 8)

